1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,357
Six doors. Easy, you counted them already.

2
00:00:03,557 --> 00:00:08,147
Now this one: an Air Force dormitory, ten thousand pixels wide.

3
00:00:08,347 --> 00:00:12,120
How many doors? Nobody should count that by eye.

4
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:14,461
So people ask a chatbot.

5
00:00:14,661 --> 00:00:19,251
You get one confident number, and nothing to check it against.

6
00:00:19,451 --> 00:00:24,041
Plumb says one hundred and thirty-nine, and boxes every single door.

7
00:00:24,241 --> 00:00:26,790
Ask in plain language. Any language.

8
00:00:26,990 --> 00:00:31,171
More doors, or toilets and sinks? One thirty-nine against ninety-six.

9
00:00:31,371 --> 00:00:35,553
Every answer comes with a receipt: measured, printed, or estimated.

10
00:00:35,753 --> 00:00:41,567
Every size starts with the scale, and Plumb reads it off the sheet itself.

11
00:00:41,767 --> 00:00:48,398
This one is a half-size print. The printed note alone would be wrong by two times.

12
00:00:48,598 --> 00:00:54,004
No scale at all? Click both ends of one door, and everything follows.

13
00:00:54,204 --> 00:00:58,794
Then it counts what's inside: forty-eight sinks, forty-eight toilets, forty-six beds.

14
00:00:58,994 --> 00:01:03,584
Code counts every symbol two ways. The model only names them.

15
00:01:03,784 --> 00:01:09,190
And it turns the drawing into a build plan: concrete, hinges, pipe, cable.

16
00:01:09,390 --> 00:01:13,163
Services use European standards, and are labeled as estimates.

17
00:01:13,363 --> 00:01:15,912
On hard sheets, it stays honest.

18
00:01:16,112 --> 00:01:23,151
This piping plan prints five check valves, but only four tags are legible. Plumb says exactly that.

19
00:01:23,351 --> 00:01:27,941
Twenty-four out of twenty-five hard questions right on Stoa's full-size sheets.

20
00:01:28,141 --> 00:01:31,098
Scale within two percent, on all three.

21
00:01:31,298 --> 00:01:36,296
And it's almost no LLM. Computer vision and formulas, on a laptop.

22
00:01:36,496 --> 00:01:41,494
Most answers cost zero tokens. Reading this whole sheet: ten cents, once.

23
00:01:41,694 --> 00:01:44,243
The model names things. Code measures.

24
00:01:44,443 --> 00:01:48,624
So next year's better model makes Plumb better, not obsolete.

25
00:01:48,824 --> 00:01:53,006
I'm Abhinav. This is Plumb: blueprints that show their work.
